+---
+
+# F-1 — CRITICAL — `deposit()` cannot work with real USDT
+
+**Where:** `src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:94` — `usdt.approve(address(aavePool), amount);`
+
+**Mechanism.** The vault calls `approve` through OpenZeppelin's `IERC20`, whose
+signature is `approve(address,uint256) returns (bool)`. Solidity therefore ABI-decodes
+32 bytes of return data. The real USDT (`TetherToken`) predates ERC-20 finalisation
+and its `approve` returns **nothing**. The decode fails and the whole transaction
+reverts. `SafeERC20` is imported and used for `safeTransferFrom` on line 93 — the very
+next line drops back to a raw `approve`.
+
+**Evidence — real mainnet state, real token, real Aave pool:**
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path 'test/fork/MainnetUsdt.t.sol' -vv
+
+Ran 5 tests for test/fork/MainnetUsdt.t.sol:MainnetUsdtForkTest
+[PASS] test_Fork_DepositRevertsOnRealUsdt() (gas: 94711)
+[FAIL: EvmError: Revert] test_Fork_DepositRevertsOnRealUsdt_RawTrace() (gas: 94227)
+[PASS] test_Fork_UsdtApproveReturnsNoData() (gas: 19064)
+Logs:
+  approve call succeeded: true
+  approve returndata length: 0
+
+[PASS] test_Fork_UsdtForbidsNonZeroToNonZeroApprove() (gas: 41704)
+Logs:
+  100 -> 200 approve succeeded: false
+
+[PASS] test_Fork_UsdtTransferReturnsNoData() (gas: 49968)
+Suite result: FAILED. 4 passed; 1 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 211.22ms (1.87ms CPU time)
+```
+
+The trace pins the revert to the instruction immediately after the `approve` returns
+with no data (`← [Stop]` = zero-length return):
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-test test_Fork_DepositRevertsOnRealUsdt_RawTrace -vvvv
+
+  [94227] MainnetUsdtForkTest::test_Fork_DepositRevertsOnRealUsdt_RawTrace()
+    ├─ [0] VM::prank(alice: [0x328809Bc894f92807417D2dAD6b7C998c1aFdac6])
+    │   └─ ← [Return]
+    ├─ [84174] UsdtYieldVault::deposit(1000000000 [1e9])
+    │   ├─ [43630] 0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7::transferFrom(alice: [0x328809Bc894f92807417D2dAD6b7C998c1aFdac6], UsdtYieldVault: [0x5615dEB798BB3E4dFa0139dFa1b3D433Cc23b72f], 1000000000 [1e9])
+    │   │   ├─ emit Transfer(from: alice: [0x328809Bc894f92807417D2dAD6b7C998c1aFdac6], to: UsdtYieldVault: [0x5615dEB798BB3E4dFa0139dFa1b3D433Cc23b72f], value: 1000000000 [1e9])
+    │   │   └─ ← [Stop]
+    │   ├─ [24953] 0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7::approve(0x87870Bca3F3fD6335C3F4ce8392D69350B4fA4E2, 1000000000 [1e9])
+    │   │   ├─ emit Approval(owner: UsdtYieldVault: [0x5615dEB798BB3E4dFa0139dFa1b3D433Cc23b72f], spender: 0x87870Bca3F3fD6335C3F4ce8392D69350B4fA4E2, value: 1000000000 [1e9])
+    │   │   └─ ← [Stop]
+    │   └─ ← [Revert] EvmError: Revert
+    └─ ← [Revert] EvmError: Revert
+
+Backtrace:
+  at UsdtYieldVault.deposit
+  at MainnetUsdtForkTest.test_Fork_DepositRevertsOnRealUsdt_RawTrace
+```
+
+The same failure reproduces offline against `UsdtLike`, a mock that mirrors USDT's
+storage-visible behaviour (`test/audit/AuditMocks.sol`):
+
+```
+[FAIL: EvmError: Revert] test_F1_DepositRevertsAgainstRealUsdtSemantics() (gas: 2340058)
+```
+
+**Second, independent trap in the same line.** USDT also forbids overwriting a
+non-zero allowance with another non-zero value. It does not bite today only because
+Aave happens to consume the full allowance each time, so it returns to zero. Any
+future path that leaves dust allowance — a partial supply, a supply-cap rejection —
+bricks every subsequent deposit. Proven above: `100 -> 200 approve succeeded: false`.
+
+**Fix.** Replace line 94 with `usdt.forceApprove(address(aavePool), amount);`
+(`SafeERC20.forceApprove` is already available via the existing `using SafeERC20`
+directive — it tolerates missing return data and resets to zero first). Better still,
+approve once in the constructor with `forceApprove` and re-arm only when the
+allowance is exhausted, saving gas per deposit. Then delete `IERC20.approve` usage
+from the contract entirely so this cannot regress.
+
+---
+
+# F-2 — CRITICAL — the yield vault delivers no yield, and the yield is unrecoverable
+
+**Where:** `src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:144` (`totalAssets()` returns
+`totalAssetsStored`), plus every write to `totalAssetsStored`.
+
+**Mechanism.** Aave V3 aTokens rebase: your aUSDT balance grows as the reserve's
+liquidity index grows. The vault records `totalAssetsStored += amount` at deposit and
+never reads `aUsdt.balanceOf(address(this))` again. In fact the `aUsdt` immutable is
+stored in the constructor and **never read anywhere in the contract** — the accounting
+was never wired to the real position.
+
+Consequences, in order of severity:
+
+- Depositors earn 0% and end up down by the 0.3% withdrawal fee.
+- The interest accumulates as aUSDT owned by the vault that no function can reach:
+  `withdraw()` only ever pulls `convertToAssets(shares)`, which is derived from the
+  same stale integer. When the last shareholder exits, `totalShares == 0` and the
+  interest is locked forever.
+
+**Evidence — the vault cannot see interest that has accrued:**
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path 'test/audit/Accounting.t.sol' -vv
+
+[FAIL: totalAssets() must track the real Aave position: 1000000000 != 1100000000] test_F2_AaveInterestNeverReachesDepositors() (gas: 256716)
+Logs:
+  vault aUSDT balance (real position): 1100000000
+  vault totalAssets()  (what it thinks): 1000000000
+  alice balanceOfUnderlying:            1000000000
+
+[FAIL: no shares exist any more, so any aUSDT left here is unrecoverable: 103000000 != 0] test_F2b_InterestIsStrandedAfterEveryoneExits() (gas: 270094)
+Logs:
+  totalShares after full exit: 0
+  aUSDT left in the vault:     103000000
+```
+
+A sole depositor supplies 1,000 USDT, Aave pays 100 USDT of interest, and the vault
+still believes it holds 1,000. After she exits, 103 USDT (100 interest + 3 fee) sits
+in the vault with zero shares outstanding.
+
+**Evidence — this is not a mock artefact; it is what mainnet Aave does.** The
+liquidity index for USDT in the real pool at `0x87870Bca…`, read at two real blocks:
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-test test_Fork_AaveIndexGrowsButVaultAccountingCannot -vv
+
+Ran 1 test for test/fork/MainnetUsdt.t.sol:MainnetAaveIndexForkTest
+[PASS] test_Fork_AaveIndexGrowsButVaultAccountingCannot() (gas: 37942)
+Logs:
+  liquidity index @ block 25_800_000: 1172551419387823837570837823
+  liquidity index @ block 26_000_000: 1175612956479947717753863949
+  elapsed seconds: 2408712
+  growth in bps over the window: 26
+  annualised bps: 340
+  USDT earned on a 1,000,000 USDT position (base units): 2600000000
+```
+
+At the rate the reserve actually paid over those 28 days — ~3.4% annualised — a
+1,000,000 USDT book strands **~2,600 USDT per month**, permanently, while the
+depositors who funded it receive nothing.
+
+**Fix.** Delete `totalAssetsStored` and make `totalAssets()` return
+`aUsdt.balanceOf(address(this))`, with `convertToShares` / `convertToAssets` reading
+from it. Two things must be done carefully alongside:
+
+- In `deposit()`, snapshot `totalAssets()` **before** the transfer-in and supply, and
+  mint shares against that snapshot; otherwise the depositor's own principal inflates
+  the denominator and they mint too few shares.
+- Balance-based accounting reopens the first-depositor inflation attack that the
+  current design is immune to (see "Cleared" below). Mitigate it the way OZ's
+  ERC4626 does — virtual shares/assets offset — or by minting a small amount of dead
+  shares to address(0) on the first deposit. Do not adopt one half of this fix
+  without the other.
+
+Once `totalAssets()` is the real balance, the withdrawal fee and the accrued interest
+both raise the share price automatically, and F-3 disappears as a side effect.
+
+---
+
+# F-3 — HIGH — the withdrawal fee is deleted, not redistributed
+
+**Where:** `src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:117` — `totalAssetsStored -= grossAssets;` while
+only `netAssets` is actually pulled out of Aave on line 119.
+
+**Mechanism.** The fee (`grossAssets - netAssets`) stays supplied in Aave, but the
+vault's accounting is decremented by the *gross* amount. The fee is therefore erased
+from the books the moment it is charged. README.md and the contract's own NatSpec
+both state the opposite: "Both fees stay supplied in Aave and accrue to the remaining
+shareholders." They do not.
+
+**Evidence:**
+
+```
+[FAIL: alice's 3 USDT withdrawal fee should accrue to the remaining holder: 999499750 < 1002399749] test_F3_WithdrawFeeDoesNotAccrueToRemainingShareholders() (gas: 355363)
+Logs:
+  bob underlying before alice's exit: 999499749
+  bob underlying after  alice's exit: 999499750
+  share price before: 1001501
+  share price after:  1001501
+
+[FAIL: 3 USDT of fee is stranded with zero shares outstanding: 3000000 != 0] test_F3b_WithdrawFeeIsStrandedForever() (gas: 257220)
+Logs:
+  totalShares:       0
+  totalAssetsStored: 0
+  aUSDT still held:  3000000
+```
+
+Alice pays a 3 USDT fee on exit. Bob, the only remaining shareholder, gains
+**1 base unit** — rounding dust, not the fee. The share price does not move at all
+(`1001501` before and after). In the single-depositor case the 3 USDT is left in the
+vault with `totalShares == 0`: unrecoverable.
+
+**Fix.** Implementing F-2 fixes this for free. If F-2 is deferred for some reason,
+the minimal change is `totalAssetsStored -= netAssets;` so the retained fee stays on
+the books and lifts the share price for everyone still in.
+
+---
+
+# F-4 — MEDIUM — small withdrawals burn shares and pay out zero
+
+**Where:** `src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:107-119`. `convertToAssets` floors, and there is no
+check that `netAssets > 0` before the shares are burned.
+
+**Evidence:**
+
+```
+[FAIL: burning shares must not return zero USDT: 0 <= 0] test_F4_DustWithdrawBurnsSharesForZeroUsdt() (gas: 259095)
+Logs:
+  shares burned: 900000000000
+  usdt received: 0
+  withdraw() returned: 0
+```
+
+900,000,000,000 shares burned, 0 USDT returned, transaction succeeds. `deposit()`
+guards the mirror case with `NoSharesMinted`; `withdraw()` has no equivalent. Any
+integrating contract that treats a successful `withdraw()` as "funds received" will
+silently lose value here.
+
+**Fix.** Add `if (netAssets == 0) revert ZeroAssets();` after computing `netAssets`
+(a new error, mirroring the existing `NoSharesMinted`). Consider also rounding
+`grossAssets` in the protocol's favour explicitly via `Math.Rounding.Floor` and
+documenting it, so the direction of rounding is a stated decision rather than an
+accident of the default.
+
+---
+
+# F-5 — HIGH — unbounded owner-set deposit fee: confiscation and denial of service
+
+**Where:** `src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:126-130` — `setDepositFee` validates nothing, and
+the constructor does not validate `_depositFeeBps` either.
+
+**Mechanism.** Because deposit fees accrue to *existing* shareholders (F-2's design
+intent), a fee raise transfers value from the incoming depositor to whoever already
+holds shares — which can be the owner. The owner sees a deposit in the mempool and
+front-runs it with `setDepositFee`.
+
+**Evidence — 99% of a deposit redirected to the existing holder:**
+
+```
+[FAIL: bob should not lose 99% of his deposit to a fee change: 19801980 < 990000000] test_F5_OwnerCanFrontRunDepositAndConfiscate99Percent() (gas: 345351)
+Logs:
+  bob deposited:             1000000000
+  bob underlying after:      19801980
+  alice underlying before:   1000000000
+  alice underlying after:    1980198019
+```
+
+Bob deposits 1,000 USDT and is credited with 19.80. Alice's position goes from
+1,000.00 to 1,980.20 — she is handed 980 USDT of Bob's money by a single owner
+transaction.
+
+**Evidence — the same setter also bricks the vault:**
+
+```
+[FAIL: panic: arithmetic underflow or overflow (0x11)] test_F5b_OwnerCanBrickDepositsWithFeeAbove100Percent() (gas: 27767)
+```
+
+`setDepositFee(10_001)` makes `fee > amount`, so `amount - fee` on line 88 underflows
+and every deposit reverts permanently.
+
+**Fix.** Introduce `uint256 public constant MAX_FEE_BPS = 100;` (1%, or whatever the
+product actually needs) and `if (newFeeBps > MAX_FEE_BPS) revert FeeTooHigh();` in
+both the setter and the constructor. Fee *increases* should additionally go through a
+timelock, so depositors can see a change before it can affect them; and ownership
+should sit behind a multisig rather than a single EOA.
+
+---
+
+# F-6 — MEDIUM/HIGH — `pause()` freezes withdrawals, not just deposits
+
+**Where:** `src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:107` — `withdraw` carries `whenNotPaused`.
+
+**Evidence:**
+
+```
+[FAIL: EnforcedPause()] test_F6_PauseTrapsDepositorFunds() (gas: 238214)
+```
+
+Alice deposits, the owner pauses, Alice can no longer withdraw her own funds. A
+single key — with no timelock, no 2-step transfer, and no guardian split — can hold
+every depositor's balance hostage indefinitely. The existing suite tests that pause
+blocks *deposits* (`test_PauseBlocksDeposit`) and never asks the question about
+withdrawals.
+
+**Fix.** Drop `whenNotPaused` from `withdraw()`. A pause should stop new risk coming
+in, never stop users getting out; that is the standard split in Aave, Compound and
+OZ's own guidance. If you genuinely need an emergency withdrawal halt, make it a
+separate, timelocked, expiring flag rather than the same switch used for routine
+maintenance.
+
+---
+
+# F-7 — MEDIUM (latent) — USDT's transfer fee would break deposits
+
+**Where:** `src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:93-95` — the vault supplies `amount` to Aave while
+only having received `amount - transferFee`.
+
+**Mechanism.** The real USDT contract contains a live, owner-settable
+`basisPointsRate` fee (max 20 bps, capped by `maximumFee`). It is 0 today. If Tether
+ever sets it, every deposit reverts, because the vault tries to supply more than it
+holds. This is tested here with a *standards-compliant* fee-charging token so the
+effect is isolated from F-1.
+
+**Evidence:**
+
+```
+[FAIL: ERC20InsufficientBalance(0xa0Cb889707d426A7A386870A03bc70d1b0697598, 998000000 [9.98e8], 999000000 [9.99e8])] test_F7_UsdtTransferFeeBreaksDeposit() (gas: 2374039)
+```
+
+Note the failure mode is a revert, not a loss — but it is a revert that neither you
+nor your depositors can fix, because `amount` is hard-wired into the supply call.
+
+**Fix.** Measure what actually arrived:
+`uint256 received = usdt.balanceOf(address(this)) - balanceBefore;` and supply and
+account `received`, not `amount`. Compute the deposit fee and the minted shares from
+`received` too.
+
+---
+
+# Stateful invariant campaign
+
+Two invariants any honest share-accounted vault must hold, driven by a handler that
+randomly deposits, withdraws and accrues Aave interest
+(`test/audit/Invariants.t.sol`):
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path 'test/audit/Invariants.t.sol'
+
+Ran 2 tests for test/audit/Invariants.t.sol:VaultInvariantTest
+[FAIL: totalAssetsStored drifted from the real aUSDT position: 199998003892 != 200632058559]
+	[Sequence] (original: 6, shrunk: 2)
+		sender=0x7E64749ad3A6d552Ef31b0529646Cc61855B5900 addr=[test/audit/Invariants.t.sol:VaultHandler]0x5991A2dF15A8F6A256D3Ec51E99254Cd3fb576A9 calldata=deposit(uint256,uint256) args=[100, 3880]
+		sender=0x00000000000000000000000000000000000003A9 addr=[test/audit/Invariants.t.sol:VaultHandler]0x5991A2dF15A8F6A256D3Ec51E99254Cd3fb576A9 calldata=accrueAaveInterest(uint256) args=[634054667 [6.34e8]]
+ invariant_AccountedAssetsMatchAavePosition() (runs: 0, calls: 0, reverts: 0)
+[PASS] invariant_SharesAreBackedByAssets() (runs: 256, calls: 128000, reverts: 0)
+```
+
+`invariant_AccountedAssetsMatchAavePosition` — the vault's books must describe the
+position it actually holds — is falsified in **two calls**. The exact shrunk sequence
+varies between runs because the fuzzer seed is random; it has failed on every run,
+always within a handful of calls. If you get a replay instead of a fresh campaign,
+`rm -rf cache/invariant` first.
+
+`invariant_SharesAreBackedByAssets` held over 128,000 calls: shares outstanding
+always have non-zero accounted assets behind them, so `convertToShares` does not
+divide by zero in practice.
+
+---
+
+# Cleared — attacks that do not work
+
+These tests pass, and they are in `test/audit/NegativeResults.t.sol` so you can see
+what was checked and ruled out, not just what was hit.
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path 'test/audit/NegativeResults.t.sol' -vv
+
+Ran 3 tests for test/audit/NegativeResults.t.sol:NegativeResultsTest
+[PASS] testFuzz_Clear_NoRoundingDrain(uint256,uint256) (runs: 256, μ: 1362112, ~: 1169053)
+[PASS] testFuzz_Clear_NoShortfallAgainstAccounting(uint256,uint256) (runs: 256, μ: 285028, ~: 285159)
+[PASS] test_Clear_DonationDoesNotMoveSharePrice() (gas: 310465)
+Logs:
+  victim shares after donation attack: 998001000000000000000
+```
+
+- **First-depositor / donation inflation attack: not exploitable.** Because the share
+  price is derived from internal accounting rather than a token balance, donating
+  500,000 USDT directly to the vault, or supplying 100,000 USDT of aUSDT to it, moves
+  the share price by exactly zero. This immunity is a *consequence* of the F-2 bug —
+  if you fix F-2 by switching to balance-based accounting, you must add the virtual
+  offset or dead shares at the same time, or you will trade F-2 for this.
+- **Rounding-based value extraction: not exploitable.** Up to 30 deposit/withdraw
+  cycles at fuzzed sizes never leave the attacker ahead, and never reduce an honest
+  holder's position. Every rounding step floors in the vault's favour.
+- **Insolvency: not present.** The real aUSDT position always covers the accounted
+  position, so no withdrawal fails for lack of funds. The drift found in F-2/F-3 is
+  always a surplus the depositors cannot reach, never a shortfall.
+
+---
+
+# Smaller items, no separate test
+
+- **`aUsdt` is write-only** (`src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:41`, set at line 76, never read).
+  It is the clearest single signal that the accounting was never connected to the
+  Aave position. Fixing F-2 gives it a purpose.
+- **`aavePool.withdraw`'s return value is discarded** (line 119). Aave returns the
+  amount actually withdrawn; it should be checked against `netAssets` and the
+  difference handled or reverted on.
+- **No zero-address validation** in the constructor for `_usdt`, `_aUsdt`,
+  `_aavePool`. A typo at deploy time produces a vault that accepts deposits into a
+  black hole.
+- **`WITHDRAW_FEE_BPS` is a constant while `depositFeeBps` is mutable**, with no
+  stated rationale. Whichever is right, make them consistent and both bounded.
+- **`Ownable`, not `Ownable2Step`.** A mistyped `transferOwnership` permanently
+  orphans `pause`, `unpause` and `setDepositFee`.
+- **Shares are not an ERC-20 and the vault is not ERC-4626.** Depositors cannot
+  transfer, compose or integrate their position. Worth a deliberate decision rather
+  than an omission — ERC-4626 would also have given you a well-trodden accounting
+  model that avoids F-2 and F-3 by construction.
+- **Testing process.** The mocks encode the assumptions under test, which is how a
+  100%-covered suite missed a contract that cannot execute a single deposit on
+  mainnet. Put the fork tests in CI against a pinned block, and keep the invariant
+  campaign — both would have caught the two criticals before code freeze.
+
+---
+
+# What I would do before Monday
+
+Fixing F-1 alone gets deposits to execute, and is the smallest change here. Do not
+let that be mistaken for readiness: a vault that executes deposits and then pays no
+yield while quietly accumulating unrecoverable interest is a worse outcome than one
+that reverts, because the reverting version loses nobody's money. F-2 is a redesign
+of the accounting core, F-5 and F-6 are governance changes, and the resulting
+contract needs a fresh review of the inflation-attack surface that the rewrite opens
+up. That is not a weekend of work, and it is not something a green test run should be
+allowed to vouch for.
